---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import AppStoreButton from '../components/AppStoreButton.astro';
import DownloadButton from '../components/DownloadButton.astro';
import { site, release, url } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="Download"
  description={`Get FinvestLens ${release.version} for ${release.platforms} — on the App Store, or for Mac as a disk image signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple.`}
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Download"
    title={`FinvestLens ${release.version}`}
    lead={`For ${release.platforms}. Free, and free software under the GPL v3.`}
  />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5 py-16">
    <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="flex flex-col rounded-2xl border border-ink-200 p-8 dark:border-ink-800">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink-900 dark:text-white">App Store</h2>
        <p class="mt-1 font-medium text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">{release.platforms}</p>
        <p class="mt-3 flex-1 text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-400">
          One free download for all three. It updates itself, and is the only way to
          get FinvestLens on iPhone and iPad.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-6"><AppStoreButton plain /></div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-500 dark:text-ink-400">{release.appStoreRequirements}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col rounded-2xl border border-ink-200 p-8 dark:border-ink-800">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink-900 dark:text-white">Disk image</h2>
        <p class="mt-1 font-medium text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">Mac only</p>
        <p class="mt-3 flex-1 text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-400">
          The same version, signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple, for
          anyone who would rather install it directly.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-6"><DownloadButton /></div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-500 dark:text-ink-400">
          {release.file} · {release.sizeLabel} · {release.requirements}
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-doc mt-14">
      <h2 id="which">Which one on a Mac?</h2>
      <p>
        Either. Both are version {release.version} from the same source, and a book
        is the same file whichever you use. They differ in two ways:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Updates.</strong> The App Store version updates itself. The disk image
          does not; a new version is announced here and on
          <a href={site.repo}>GitHub</a>, and you install it over the old one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Private Cloud Compute.</strong> Apple offers it only to apps from the
          App Store, so the disk image runs Apple Intelligence on your Mac alone, or a
          model of your own. On the App Store version it stays off unless you allow it
          (see <a href={url('/privacy/')}>the privacy policy</a>).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Install one or the other: they are the same app to macOS.</p>

      <h2>Installing the disk image</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Open the downloaded disk image.</li>
        <li>Drag <strong>FinvestLens</strong> onto the Applications folder.</li>
        <li>Open it from Applications.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Coming from GnuCash? On a Mac, choose <strong>File ▸ Import GnuCash…</strong>
        and pick your <code>.gnucash</code> file — accounts, transactions, prices, schedules
        and business records all come across, and you can export back at any time. The
        book it makes opens on your iPhone and iPad too.
      </p>
      <p>
        Coming from the 1.1 disk image? {release.version} runs in the App Sandbox, as
        the App Store requires, so open each book once with <strong>File ▸ Open</strong>,
        a drag or the Finder; from then on it reopens by itself. The same goes for the
        two document folders in Settings.
      </p>

      <h2 id="signed">Signed and notarized</h2>
      <p>
        The disk image is signed with Hello Tham’s Apple Developer ID and submitted to
        Apple for notarization, so macOS opens it without a Gatekeeper warning. You can
        check that for yourself before you install:
      </p>
      <pre><code>spctl --assess --type open --context context:primary-signature -vv {release.file}</code></pre>
      <p>It should report:</p>
      <pre><code>source=Notarized Developer ID
origin={release.signedBy}</code></pre>
      <p>And to verify the download arrived intact:</p>
      <pre><code>shasum -a 256 {release.file}</code></pre>
      <p class="break-all"><code>{release.sha256}</code></p>

      <h2 id="older">On an older Mac</h2>
      <p>
        FinvestLens {release.version} needs macOS 27 and Apple silicon.
        <a href={release.previous.url}>FinvestLens {release.previous.version}</a> stays
        on GitHub for {release.previous.requirements}.
      </p>

      <h2 id="whats-new">What’s in {release.version}</h2>
      <p>
        On the App Store since {release.released}. The headlines; the
        <a href={release.notes}>release notes</a> have the rest.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Books on Box, with nothing to set up.</strong> Choose Open from Box…, sign
          in on Box’s own page, and your books are there. There is no longer a Box app of
          your own to register, and FinvestLens never sees your Box password.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Report tables.</strong> Every second row is shaded across the whole width
          of the table, as GnuCash shades it, and long reports scroll smoothly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Price History.</strong> Buys and sales are marked in the same shapes and
          colours in the chart and in its key, the key fits a phone screen, and VoiceOver
          names each buy and sale.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A security’s page.</strong> The chart’s key lists only what the chart
          draws, and VoiceOver reads each figure with its name.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reload and Revert.</strong> The saved book is read in full before anything
          is replaced, so a file damaged or half-written by a sync can no longer take the
          place of your work. A failed save, reload or revert says which it was.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Shortcuts and Spotlight</strong> read your book from a copy, so a save
          that lands while they are reading no longer cuts them off.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The command line</h2>
      <p>
        <code>finlens</code> is a read-only, ledger-modelled reporter over your book.
        It is safe to run while the app has the book open — it takes no lock and
        never writes. Build it from source:
      </p>
      <pre><code>swift build -c release --package-path Packages/CLI

finlens -f Book.finvestlens bal ^Assets ^Liabilities
finlens -f Book.finvestlens reg Groceries -p "last month"</code></pre>
      <p>
        Full details are in <a href={url('/manual/interchange/')}>the manual</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Source code</h2>
      <p>
        FinvestLens is free software. The complete source is on
        <a href={site.repo}>GitHub</a>, and you are welcome to build it yourself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
